---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Writing useful title tags — AISEOOnline"
  description="Learn how to write distinctive, descriptive HTML page titles without relying on rigid character-count rules."
  type="article"
>
  <article class="section-space bg-white">
    <div class="site-container max-w-3xl">
      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="text-sm text-slate-600">
        <a class="inline-link" href="/guides/">Guides</a><span class="mx-2" aria-hidden="true"
          >/</span
        ><span aria-current="page">Title tags</span>
      </nav>
      <p class="eyebrow mt-12">On-page SEO</p>
      <h1 class="section-title mt-4">Writing useful page titles</h1>
      <p class="page-intro">
        A good HTML title tells readers and search engines what a page is about, without repeating
        the same generic phrase across your whole site.
      </p>
      <div class="prose-article mt-12">
        <h2>Start with the page's purpose</h2>
        <p>
          Describe what someone can expect to find. A specific product, service, or article title is
          usually more useful than a broad collection of keywords.
        </p>
        <h2>Write unique, readable titles</h2>
        <p>
          Pages with distinct content should generally have distinct titles. Keep important terms
          natural, avoid repetitive wording, and add your brand where it helps readers recognize the
          source.
        </p>
        <h2>Length is a guideline, not a guarantee</h2>
        <p>
          Search engines may shorten or rewrite displayed titles depending on the query and page
          content. A character count can flag unusually long titles, but no fixed number guarantees
          how a result will appear.
        </p>
        <h2>Check the rendered page</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Verify that the HTML title matches the purpose of the page.</li><li>
            Look for missing, duplicate, or misleading titles.
          </li><li>Ensure the prominent page heading and content support the title.</li><li>
            Check that templates do not accidentally generate identical titles site-wide.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Title tags are one part of a page's search presentation. They are not a substitute for
          accurate content, accessible navigation, or sound technical foundations.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a
        class="mt-10 inline-block font-semibold text-teal-800 underline underline-offset-4"
        href="/guides/">← Back to guides</a
      >
    </div>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
